<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Meeting My Friends – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize that people are speaking in English. Interactive: Respond to basic greetings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize their name when called in English and polite words like "please" and "thank you" (e.g., turning their head, making eye contact, or saying "Yes" when their name is called; smiling or pausing when hearing "thank you").
                Can respond to greetings and polite words in English, such as "hello," "goodbye," "please," and "thank you" (e.g., saying "hello" when greeted, saying "thank you" when given something, or using "please" when prompted).
                Can follow basic instructions paired with gestures, such as "say hello" and "wave" (e.g., waving when asked).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage &amp; Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities &amp; Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Hello Song" with TPR. Students stand in a circle. The teacher sings "Hello, hello, hello! How are you?" and waves (verb: wave). Students wave back and listen as the teacher calls out their names: "Hello, [Student's Name]!" This activity introduces "hello" and listening for names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher uses a friendly puppet (e.g., a "friend" puppet) and greets each student individually, saying "Hello! My name is Teacher [Teacher's Name]. What is your name?" The teacher models waving (verb: wave) and a happy facial expression (adjective: happy). The teacher clearly pronounces "hello" (interjection: hello) and emphasizes the initial /f/ in "friend" when introducing the puppet as a "new friend" (noun: friend). The teacher also uses "please" and "thank you" in simple requests, e.g., "Please sit down."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher waves) "Is this 'hello'?" (Students nod or say "Yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to self) "Am I a teacher?" (Students nod or say "Yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a happy face) "Am I happy?" (Students nod or say "Yes").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Simple audio dialogue with character voices and sound effects (e.g., children laughing, voices saying "hello", birds chirping). The audio features two children meeting for the first time in a playground. The dialogue is short and repetitive, focusing on greetings and names: "Hello! My name is Lily. What is your name? My name is Tom. Nice to meet you, Tom!" (Grammar: Subject + Verb: "My name is...", WH-question: "What is your name?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner recognizes and comprehends basic words, phrases, and simple statements related to personal identification and greetings, specifically distinguishing names and polite phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio. Students listen and point to flashcards representing "Lily" and "Tom" (or generic boy/girl flashcards). The teacher asks, "Who says 'hello'?" or "Who is Lily?" (Students point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen for Keywords:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students raise their hand or clap when they hear "hello" (interjection: hello) or "name" (noun: name).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Emotional Connection:</b> After listening, the teacher asks, "Are Lily and Tom happy to meet?" (adjective: happy) and models a happy face for students to imitate, linking tone of voice to emotion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill &amp; Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + Verb ("I am [name]."), WH-questions ("What is your name?"), Yes/No Questions ("Are you Mariana?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> friend, teacher, name, class, say, is, play, wave, meet, nice, happy, please, hello, goodbye, thank you.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Syllable stress in "friends" and "goodbye"; phonemic awareness: Initial /f/ in "friend," /g/ in "goodbye," and environmental sounds (e.g., voices saying "hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Using polite expressions: Saying "please" and "thank you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Politeness in greetings: Saying "hello," "goodbye," "please," "thank you."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point to Names (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays large flashcards with students' names. The teacher calls out a student's name: "Hello, [Student's Name]!" and the entire class listens and points to the correct name card. The named student waves. The teacher repeats with several names, focusing on clear pronunciation and listening for individual recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Respond to Greetings (Semi-Controlled Drill):</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of various voices saying "Hello!" or "Goodbye!" or "Thank you!" Students listen and respond with the appropriate gesture: wave for "hello" (verb: wave), wave back for "goodbye" (interjection: goodbye), or give a thumbs-up for "thank you" (interjection: thank you). The teacher also practices "please" by saying "Please clap your hands" or "Please stand up."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Identify (Pair Interaction/Peer Check - Guided):</b> Students are paired up. Each student has a small picture card (e.g., a "friend" or a "teacher"). The teacher says, "Listen: Who is the friend?" (noun: friend). Students point to the "friend" card. Then the teacher says, "Listen: Who is the teacher?" (noun: teacher). Students point. They then turn to their partner and show them the card they pointed to. This reinforces listening for specific vocabulary.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided "Meet and Greet Circle." Students stand in a large circle. The teacher initiates by walking to a student, saying "Hello, [Student's Name]! I am Teacher [Teacher's Name]. What is your name?" The student is encouraged to respond "Hello! I am [their name]" (Subject + Verb: "I am..."). The student then turns to the next person and attempts to greet them. The teacher provides support by repeating or prompting. The focus is on listening to the greeting and the question, then producing their name. The teacher reminds them to say "please" and "thank you" if they need help or receive a card. Syllable stress in "friends" and the initial /f/ will be reinforced by the teacher's modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This activity directly simulates meeting new friends in the classroom, encouraging real-life application of greetings and name exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in responding to basic greetings ("hello") and self-identifying by name ("I am..."), as well as listening for peers' names and using polite expressions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Meet and Greet Circle" and a quick "Listen and Act" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and respond to their name when called by the teacher or a peer (e.g., turning their head, making eye contact, saying "Yes" or "Hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and follow a basic instruction paired with a gesture (e.g., "wave goodbye," "say hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen for and react to "please" and "thank you" (e.g., pausing when hearing "thank you" after receiving a crayon).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist - Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes own name when called (e.g., turns head, makes eye contact).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Hello" (e.g., says "hello" back, waves).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Goodbye" (e.g., waves back).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows recognition of "please" and "thank you" (e.g., pauses or smiles).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows simple, gestured instructions like "wave."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher shows two pictures: one with children saying "hello" happily, another with children saying "goodbye." The teacher asks, "Did you hear 'hello' today?" (Students point to the "hello" picture or give a thumbs-up). "Did you hear 'goodbye'?" (Students point to "goodbye" or thumbs-up).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> (Teacher asks aloud, students respond with gestures or simple words)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like listening to new English words today?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What English words did you hear today?" (Students may point to their mouths for "say hello" or wave for "goodbye").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote listening skills for Pre-K students? (e.g., TPR, repetitive audio, clear modeling, individual name calling). The use of puppets and direct interaction seemed highly engaging and effective for initial recognition of "hello" and names. Students responded well to the "Hello Song."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle to differentiate between "hello" and "goodbye" solely by sound; more visual cues and physical actions are needed. Attention span for audio-only tasks is short, so frequent interaction and movement are critical. I will need to provide more explicit reinforcement of "please" and "thank you" paired with object exchange.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students will focus on Reading Comprehension, using flashcards and simple picture stories to visually recognize the vocabulary they have just heard, such as "friend," "name," "hello," and "goodbye," reinforcing the connection between spoken word and written/pictured representation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr></tbody></table>